  - [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mB. Transactional outbox.[0m Task writes append a [38;5;153mtask_events[39m row in the same transaction. A separate fan-out step reads
  events and creates notifications.
  - [3mWins:[0m the write path stays constant-time. Retries have an obvious home. And the crucial one — [1mthe event log is task
[0m    [1mactivity history[0m, which a task app wants regardless ("Sam changed status to Done, 2h ago"). You get a feature you'd
    otherwise build separately.
  - [3mCosts:[0m eventual consistency (notification lands a beat after the change), and a fan-out runner to operate. Two
    concepts where A has one.
  - [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  [1mC. Database triggers + [38;5;153mLISTEN/NOTIFY[39m.[0m Postgres detects the change itself.
  - [3mWins:[0m impossible to bypass; no application code can forget to emit an event.
  - [3mCosts:[0m business logic in PL/pgSQL, awkward to test, awkward to version, and any future non-Postgres path is dead.
    [1mDiscarded[0m — the bypass-proofing isn't worth losing testability on a greenfield app.

  [1mRecommendation: B, with the fan-out running in-process on an interval to start.[0m Not because of scale — you don't have
  scale — but because the event log pays for itself twice, as notifications [3mand[0m as activity history, and because
  starting with A means the change-detection logic gets born inside the update handler where it's hardest to pull back
  out. B's extra cost right now is roughly one table and one function.

  The in-process runner keeps it honest: no queue, no Redis, no worker deployment until something actually demands it.
